Subject: What Is Perspective Of OpenBSD/Alpha ?
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=104912104500540

Hello,

    I would like to run a secure server (primary DNS and a couple of 
secondaries, DHCP, TACACS+, RADIUS, LDAP) on Alphaserver 800. I've been 
using Digital Unix 4.0 since 1998 quite happily, but its support is over 
for some time and there were recently some serious vulnerabilities 
reported. As support for 5.1a will be terminated within one year, it's 
time to upgrade to other OS. "Secure" implies OpenBSD naturally as well 
as Alpha does ( 
http://marc.theaimsgroup.com/?l=openbsd-tech&m=104391783312978&q=raw ). :-)

    http://www.openbsd.org/alpha.html is, however, quite depressive page.

Could somebody take a crystall ball and try to prophet, whether 
OpenBSD/Alpha will be supported within next three to five years or if I 
should move on to FreeBSD, NetBSD, Debian Linux because there are too 
few people generally interested?


Thanks in advance,

Ivan Dolezal

